 ],
 "text": "What if the internet went down tomorrow, everywhere, for 30 days? No Wi-Fi, no mobile data, no cloud. Just silence. Hour 1. Your card stops working at the coffee shop. The barista shrugs. Only 14% of payments in the US are still made in cash, according to the Federal Reserve, and you're not one of them. Day 1. More than 99% of the data between continents travels through cables on the seafloor. They're still there. Nothing is moving through them. So banks, airlines, and hospitals go back to paper. Pilots get printed flight plans, doctors write prescriptions by hand again, and nobody can read them. Day 3. Stores reorder by phone and fax. Shelves start to thin out, starting with bread, batteries, and toilet paper. Week 2. Neighbors trade favors. Somebody's grandad becomes the most important person on the street because he still has a radio and knows how to use it. Day 30. The internet comes back on. Billions of people log in at once, and nobody remembers their passwords. Would you last 30 days offline? Follow for part 2."
}